{
 "about": "Crowning cartouche of the Garden Gate (plate 4, 'North Side of Garden Gate'), left half, traced on the Palace Museum copy (album_2.jpg, public domain) in its own pixels: the cap with its coronet, the palmette, the outer frame with its feet and the inner C-scrolls. Bands are raised straps of the given width along a Catmull-Rom centre line; closed shapes are raised forms; ground_at marks the hatched (sunk) fields. The right half is the mirror image about x 2482, the attic's axis (both halves of the frame fit best about 2482, the cap about 2482.5). The eave polyline runs about 5 px under the attic eave's top, from the axis into the frame's foot, and closes the crown's body against it (the feet stand outside it). frame_inner is the narrow strip running inside the frame, with the groove frame_groove between, along its slope and side (self-check 2026-11-28: the album shows the frame as a broad band, a groove and a narrow strip; the model had the broad band only). Self-check 2026-11-28 on a 1-px brightness map of the album: the shell is a cluster of solid round lobes on the sunk ground, edged on each side by a thin strap (shell_edge). The lobes (lobe_a-d, and lobe_m on the axis) are the album's own outlines: the iso-brightness 144 contour of each lobe on the 0.8-px-smoothed scan, resampled to 12 points (a bead is as high as a carved form, so beads on a raised shell plate vanished). The boss is a shield pointed at the top, half-width 6.5 px over rows 1036-1039; the collar is half-width 4 px over rows 1059.5-1064.5. The fan's top leaf is pointed (album rows 1022-1026, dark notches either side), so its apex points are close together: drawn as a half shape its end tangent is level, and widely spaced points rounded it into a knob.",
